Being a bad player (Demigod, but blows up 66% of BoH's) it seems pretty
dangerous to me :-)

If good players exploit it *perfectly*, what do they get that they
don't already get from other places? +5 armor, and +7 weapons? Don't
most players already have far easier and safer ways of getting the same
thing? It's like confusing yourself by staring at an umber hulk instead
of just drinking some booze.

To properly exploit it you'd have to
1: get a monster somewhere you can safely shuffle your equipment
2: confuse it
3: make sure you're not wearing or wielding anything you don't want
over-enchanted
4: make sure, while you're lesserly equipped, nothing bad shows up to
ruin your day
5: make sure the thing you do want to get enchanted doesn't get
over-enchanted
6: make sure the monster doesn't get un-confused and go back to its
normal attack.
7: if you're trying to "dance" with 8 R's, make sure all 8 of them are
properly confused and they don't all enchant at once, which would
vaporize anything +1 or higher.

Yeah, a good player could probably work that to great advantage.  But
there's so many variables to juggle, I think it adds more potential
downside (rusty,  rotted, negative-enchanted, or vaporized armor, Naked
or semi-equipped Lich / slime / cockatrice / Demogorgon attacks,
ignominous death, etc.) than upside (+5 everything, which any
fair-to-middle skilled player can get in many ways already).

At least in my mind, it doesn't take anything away from the game by
making it "too easy" and it adds an interesting gameplay twist in that
even if you are shamelessly spoiled and fully aware of the
consequences, (which I'd say most demigods are) you can still screw
yourself up pretty bad by bad luck or not being paranoid enough.



